Park posts the provider keeps refusing instead of spinning on them forever.

EFA must hear a peer's handshake before it posts an RMA to that peer. Until it
does, it queues up to 16 operations per endpoint and refuses the rest with
-FI_EAGAIN, and a peer that stopped or died before answering never answers.
libfabric 2.4 lowers its count of queued operations on every progress pass
over them, so up to 16 more get in before the count wraps, and from then on it
refuses every post to a peer whose handshake has not arrived. NIXL 1.5.0's
progress thread retries a refused post in place forever, reaping only its own
rail's completions in between. One read or write to such a peer therefore
stops the agent outright, because the thread never gets back to its other
posts, its other rails or their completions. Every later transfer on the agent
then reports zero of its requests complete with no CQ error, and once a rail's
post ring fills the caller blocks in MpscRing::push with the agent lock held.

The progress thread now retries a refused post in place for a short budget,
which usually covers a send queue that is only full, then parks it behind the
posts already parked for its destination and moves on. Each pass first serves
the parked destinations one post at a time in rounds, starting after the
destination that took the last post, so a busy destination cannot take every
slot the provider frees. A destination that takes no post for
NIXL_LIBFABRIC_POST_RETRY_TIMEOUT_MS (2000 ms by default, 0 to wait forever)
has all of its parked posts failed the way a failed post already is. That is
far longer than any refusal a live peer causes, the handshake round trip of a
healthy new peer included, and well inside the five seconds a KV cache
connector gives a transfer, so a transfer whose posts all fail ends in an
error first. A refusal that covers the whole rail, such as a peer that leaves
the device retrying writes it never takes, fails every destination's parked
posts after the same bound instead of stalling them. Parked posts left on a
rail with no registrations are failed rather than tried again. A rail parks at
most one post ring's worth of posts. At that limit a post to a destination
that has taken none for 100 ms fails at once instead of parking, and a refused
post that needs room first fails the parked posts of such destinations, so one
silent peer's backlog cannot keep the rail from taking other destinations'
posts. Only when no room frees does the rail hold the post back and stop
taking new ones from the ring, so the threads pushing to it wait at the pace
the provider takes posts, as they did before. Posts failed at the limit are
reported in at most one line a second per rail. A parked post goes without
FI_MORE, and so does the next post after one is set aside while a batch is
open, so the next post the provider takes on the rail rings the doorbell for
the writes it holds. The first post a parked destination takes logs, at INFO,
how long after its first refusal it came.

A transfer whose refused posts all fail completes with an error. The up to 32
operations EFA queued before the refusals stay with the provider and complete
only if the peer answers, so a transfer that includes them can stay pending.
Its caller needs its own deadline for it, and after giving up on it must
neither deregister nor reuse its memory, because the operations EFA holds may
still write it.

postSend, which sends handshakes and notifications, and the postRead and
postWrite loops that run when the progress thread is off retried -FI_EAGAIN
forever too, and now give up after the same bound. A handshake reply the
progress thread sends itself reaps nothing while it waits, so a full rail 0
send queue costs the agent up to the bound there instead of a permanent stall.
The drain interleave's completion poll also failed to report an error entry to
its request, which left that transfer's count short forever, and now fails the
request the way progressCompletionQueue() does.

With a silent peer no longer freezing it, the progress thread also reaps the
provider's own error when that peer's endpoint is later destroyed, for example
on the packet that asked it for its handshake. With no event queue bound the
provider aborts the process on such an error, so each rail now binds one and
drains it on every pass. An error that a lost peer explains (FI_ENOTCONN,
FI_ECONNABORTED or FI_EHOSTUNREACH) is logged, and any other still aborts.

